---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import CTABanner from '../components/CTABanner.astro';
---

<PageLayout
  title="About 1404 — A transformational technology partner"
  description="1404 is a technology partner for owner-operated companies. Forward-deployed engineers who build and run an AI strategy alongside your team. Founded in Detroit."
>
  <section class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6 pt-40 pb-24 md:pb-32">

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="mb-20">
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl lg:text-6xl text-text-primary leading-tight">
        Why 1404 Exists
      </h1>
    </header>

    <!-- The Motive -->
    <div class="mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">The Motive</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Growing up in Detroit, you see the pattern. Manufacturing floors that run on tribal knowledge. Construction firms where the most critical processes live in someone's head, or worse, in a spreadsheet no one else understands. Landscaping companies, auto repair chains, dental practices. Companies doing $5M, $20M, $100M in revenue with technology that belongs in 2008.
        </p>
        <p>
          It isn't laziness or ignorance. It's a market failure. The people who build technology don't want to work in these industries, and the people who run these companies can't find the people who build technology. The bridge between those two worlds simply doesn't exist.
        </p>
        <p>
          The tools are here now, and most companies still can't get the return. MIT's 2025 study found that about 95% of company AI efforts never move the bottom line. Not because the technology is weak, but because a stack of tools is not a strategy. Closing that gap takes someone who builds the strategy around how your business actually runs, and stays to run it with you. That is why 1404 exists.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The Mechanism -->
    <div class="mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">The Mechanism</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          1404 works in two parts. It starts with a Foundation. We come on-site, put the best AI tools in your team's hands and get them fluent, connect the systems you already run so they work as one, and build a clear plan from your growth goals.
        </p>
        <p>
          Then it becomes an ongoing Partnership on a flat monthly retainer. We keep building whatever moves the business forward, from AI agents that handle the busywork to custom software for any opportunity, and we share in the upside we create. We're forward-deployed, working alongside your team, so the strategy and the building happen in the same place.
        </p>
        <p>
          The learning curve is weeks, not years, because we've already built the architecture patterns and industry knowledge that a new hire would spend their first year developing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Why It Matters -->
    <div class="mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">Why It Matters</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Give one person a shovel and another person an excavator. Come back in a week. The difference between them isn't two or ten times, it's a different category of output entirely. That's what technology does to operations. The company with real-time labor forecasting doesn't just schedule a bit better than the company using spreadsheets. They're operating at a fundamentally different level. The company with AI watching every workflow doesn't just catch problems faster, it prevents problems the other company doesn't even know it has.
        </p>
        <p>
          And the advantage builds on itself. Every month the system learns more. Every quarter the institutional knowledge deepens. After two years, it knows things about your operations that no single person in your company knows. After five years, the gap between you and the company that didn't invest is hard to close.
        </p>
        <p>
          None of that is speculative. It's just what happens when a system learns continuously and the knowledge accumulates over years instead of resetting every time someone leaves.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Backed by TCG -->
    <div class="border-t border-border pt-16 mb-20">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-6">Backed by The Construct Group</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6 text-text-body text-base leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          We built our systems and agent architecture inside a real operating company, The Construct Group, a national retail fixture installation company. We built their entire technology from scratch. A 180,000-line unified operations platform, AI agents running daily operations, and ongoing technology leadership. The results were significant enough that TCG came on as an equity partner.
        </p>
        <p>
          That partnership means something specific. It means TCG believed in what we were building enough to put real capital behind it, and it means 1404 has been pressure-tested inside a real operating company with real revenue, real employees, and real complexity before we ever took on a second client. The systems we deploy and the process we follow were all developed in production, not in a lab.
        </p>
        <p>
          TCG still runs on 1404's platform, and Ben Barnes, TCG's CEO, actively introduces 1404 to other operators in his network. Since then we've expanded beyond construction into landscaping, auto repair, and financial services. The underlying approach works across industries because the operational problems are structurally similar even when the specifics are different.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Cofounders -->
    <div class="border-t border-border pt-16">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl md:text-3xl text-text-primary mb-10">Cofounders</h2>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-8">
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl md:text-2xl text-text-primary mb-1">Arthur Skatoff</h3>
          <p class="text-xs tracking-widest uppercase text-text-muted mb-3">Cofounder</p>
          <a href="mailto:arthur@1404.io" class="text-copper text-sm hover:text-copper-bright transition-colors duration-200">arthur@1404.io</a>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-display text-xl md:text-2xl text-text-primary mb-1">Holden Bryce</h3>
          <p class="text-xs tracking-widest uppercase text-text-muted mb-3">Cofounder</p>
          <a href="mailto:holden@1404.io" class="text-copper text-sm hover:text-copper-bright transition-colors duration-200">holden@1404.io</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CTABanner />
</PageLayout>
